WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:13.690
Therapeutic hypothermia, now usually called targeted temperature management, is careful control of body temperature to protect the brain from secondary injury after cardiac arrest.

2
00:00:13.690 --> 00:00:43.000
In this chapter we cover the current targets from the instructor's TH slides and how they differ from older numbers, the evidence behind cooling, how cooling protects the brain, why it is not routine in traumatic brain injury, the three phases of cooling, shivering, cold diuresis, the potassium and glucose swings, the ABG at thirty seven degrees, and the respiratory therapist's ventilator targets.

3
00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:52.398
The instructor's slides give the current numbers, marked as twenty twenty five AHA updates.

4
00:00:52.398 --> 00:00:59.078
Who: adults who remain unresponsive after return of spontaneous circulation.

5
00:00:59.078 --> 00:01:09.186
Temperature: select and keep a constant temperature between thirty two and thirty seven point five degrees Celsius.

6
00:01:09.186 --> 00:01:12.351
How long: at least thirty six hours.

7
00:01:12.351 --> 00:01:30.369
Alongside it, avoid hypotension, using fluids and vasopressors to a mean arterial pressure of at least sixty five, individualized to the patient, and avoid hypoxemia, excess oxygen, and large PaCO2 swings.

8
00:01:30.369 --> 00:01:35.907
The primary goal is to protect the brain from secondary injury.

9
00:01:35.907 --> 00:01:45.575
Modern practice stresses controlled temperature and fever prevention, not cooling every patient to thirty two.

10
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:53.905
You will see several temperature ranges, so match the number to the question.

11
00:01:53.905 --> 00:02:05.474
For a current practice question, use the slide text: thirty two to thirty seven point five degrees for at least thirty six hours.

12
00:02:05.474 --> 00:02:17.043
If the question names the Hypothermia After Cardiac Arrest trial, answer thirty two to thirty four degrees for twenty four hours.

13
00:02:17.043 --> 00:02:22.692
The RCP 105 slide also prints thirty two to thirty four for TH.

14
00:02:22.692 --> 00:02:27.714
The older TTM range is thirty two to thirty six degrees.

15
00:02:27.714 --> 00:02:34.530
The RCP 105 notes give that range, and Hess adds at least twenty four hours.

16
00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:41.525
Hess defines hypothermia as a core temperature of thirty five degrees or less.

17
00:02:42.933 --> 00:02:58.537
The landmark Hypothermia After Cardiac Arrest trial in two thousand two enrolled two hundred seventy five patients resuscitated from ventricular fibrillation or ventricular tachycardia arrest.

18
00:02:58.537 --> 00:03:05.445
The cooled group was held at thirty two to thirty four degrees for twenty four hours.

19
00:03:05.445 --> 00:03:09.752
Survival was sixty four percent versus fifty percent.

20
00:03:09.752 --> 00:03:22.430
Favorable neurologic outcome was forty seven percent versus thirty one percent, and the notes call that sixteen point difference the most important finding.

21
00:03:22.430 --> 00:03:27.062
Favorable means Cerebral Performance Category one or two.

22
00:03:27.062 --> 00:03:38.683
Separately, in early studies, the benefit was clearest in ventricular fibrillation; the PEA and asystole groups were small and less conclusive.

23
00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:48.638
Hypoxic ischemic encephalopathy is damage to the brain and spinal cord from severe cerebral hypoxia.

24
00:03:48.638 --> 00:03:51.028
The deck names three causes.

25
00:03:51.028 --> 00:03:59.651
One, inadequate ventilation and oxygenation, such as respiratory arrest or carbon monoxide poisoning.

26
00:03:59.651 --> 00:04:04.688
Two, inadequate perfusion, such as cardiac arrest or shock.

27
00:04:04.688 --> 00:04:14.165
Three, a fall in cerebral perfusion pressure from a low mean arterial pressure or a high intracranial pressure.

28
00:04:14.165 --> 00:04:25.264
Treatments listed include oxygenation, ventilation and perfusion, seizure control, hyperbaric oxygen, and therapeutic hypothermia.

29
00:04:25.264 --> 00:04:30.386
Routine hyperbaric oxygen for HIE is still being researched.

30
00:04:30.386 --> 00:04:44.900
After return of circulation, the respiratory therapist maintains oxygenation and ventilation and supports temperature management while avoiding hypotension and hypoxemia.

31
00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:57.217
When cerebral blood flow stops, neuronal oxygen stores are used up in about twenty seconds and the patient loses consciousness.

32
00:04:57.217 --> 00:05:03.320
Within five minutes of complete anoxia, brain glucose and ATP are gone.

33
00:05:03.320 --> 00:05:09.767
Then the sodium potassium pump fails and the neuronal membrane depolarizes.

34
00:05:09.767 --> 00:05:17.847
Depolarized neurons release excess glutamate, the brain's primary excitatory neurotransmitter.

35
00:05:17.847 --> 00:05:27.818
Glutamate opens channels that let calcium flood in, which switches on destructive enzymes and nitric oxide synthase.

36
00:05:27.818 --> 00:05:33.577
Chloride and sodium follow, osmotic edema forms, and the cell dies.

37
00:05:33.577 --> 00:05:45.525
The instructor's one line version is energy failure, calcium overload, neuronal death, and the glutamate step is the concept worth knowing.

38
00:05:46.933 --> 00:05:51.406
The TH slides list how cooling may protect the brain.

39
00:05:51.406 --> 00:05:54.528
It suppresses inflammatory mediators.

40
00:05:54.528 --> 00:05:59.423
It reduces free radical production and helps mitochondria.

41
00:05:59.423 --> 00:06:04.233
It may reduce caspase activation, which limits apoptosis.

42
00:06:04.233 --> 00:06:14.697
It stabilizes neuron membranes and may reduce glutamate release and calcium influx while lowering the brain's oxygen demand.

43
00:06:14.697 --> 00:06:19.423
It may protect the blood brain barrier and reduce edema.

44
00:06:19.423 --> 00:06:31.744
It prevents fever, which the notes call the most clinically important row, because fever raises metabolic demand and lowers the seizure threshold.

45
00:06:31.744 --> 00:06:38.326
Brain metabolism falls about five to seven percent for each degree of cooling.

46
00:06:38.326 --> 00:06:44.908
The deck stresses these are proposed mechanisms, not proof of better outcomes.

47
00:06:46.333 --> 00:06:51.986
An ischemic cell can recover, die by necrosis, or enter apoptosis.

48
00:06:51.986 --> 00:06:59.694
Necrosis is uncontrolled death from severe injury, with membrane rupture and inflammation.

49
00:06:59.694 --> 00:07:02.692
Apoptosis is programmed cell death.

50
00:07:02.692 --> 00:07:14.854
It can happen hours or days later and adds to delayed brain damage, but the cells shrink and are cleared without the inflammation of necrosis.

51
00:07:14.854 --> 00:07:19.308
Brain injury does not stop when circulation returns.

52
00:07:19.308 --> 00:07:35.667
The slides describe post resuscitation encephalopathy: ischemia and cell injury lead to loss of autoregulation and microcirculatory dysfunction, creating an oxygen supply and demand mismatch.

53
00:07:35.667 --> 00:07:46.458
With autoregulation impaired, brain flow follows systemic blood pressure, so hypotension after resuscitation worsens ischemia.

54
00:07:47.867 --> 00:08:00.008
Cerebral autoregulation keeps brain blood flow steady across a wide range of blood pressures by constricting and dilating cerebral vessels.

55
00:08:00.008 --> 00:08:07.432
That is why not everyone with a blood pressure of ninety over sixty becomes ischemic.

56
00:08:07.432 --> 00:08:18.874
Autoregulation can be lost in severe hypotension or head trauma, and then flow depends on blood pressure and intracranial pressure.

57
00:08:18.874 --> 00:08:26.386
Cerebral perfusion pressure equals mean arterial pressure minus intracranial pressure.

58
00:08:26.386 --> 00:08:39.138
For the HIE deck, normal CPP is seventy to eighty with a critical threshold of seventy, and normal ICP is eight to twelve with twenty as critical.

59
00:08:39.138 --> 00:08:46.649
Mortality rises about twenty percent for every ten millimeters of mercury drop in CPP.

60
00:08:46.649 --> 00:08:51.017
For trauma questions, the goal is CPP above sixty.

61
00:08:52.433 --> 00:08:57.697
A head injured patient has a mean arterial pressure of ninety.

62
00:08:57.697 --> 00:09:02.026
The intracranial pressure rises from ten to thirty.

63
00:09:02.026 --> 00:09:14.591
Compute the cerebral perfusion pressure before and after, and decide whether each value meets the trauma goal and the HIE deck's critical threshold.

64
00:09:14.591 --> 00:09:16.458
Pause and work it out.

65
00:09:17.367 --> 00:09:23.367
[6-second practice pause]

66
00:09:23.867 --> 00:09:33.937
Before, ninety minus ten is eighty, which meets both the trauma goal of above sixty and the HIE threshold of seventy.

67
00:09:33.937 --> 00:09:37.036
After, ninety minus thirty is sixty.

68
00:09:37.036 --> 00:09:47.193
The trauma goal is greater than sixty, so sixty no longer meets it, and it is well below the HIE threshold of seventy.

69
00:09:47.193 --> 00:09:53.993
The intracranial pressure of thirty is also above the critical value of twenty.

70
00:09:53.993 --> 00:10:00.449
Raise the mean arterial pressure, lower the intracranial pressure, or both.

71
00:10:00.449 --> 00:10:10.692
With mortality rising about twenty percent per ten millimeters of mercury drop, this fall from eighty to sixty matters.

72
00:10:12.100 --> 00:10:16.387
Cardiac arrest is a global loss of brain blood flow.

73
00:10:16.387 --> 00:10:27.022
Traumatic brain injury is a direct mechanical injury, often regional, with edema, raised intracranial pressure, and inflammation.

74
00:10:27.022 --> 00:10:41.943
Induced hypothermia may lower intracranial pressure, but it has not consistently improved neurologic outcome in brain trauma, so routine prophylactic hypothermia is not recommended.

75
00:10:41.943 --> 00:10:46.313
Fever prevention and adequate perfusion still matter.

76
00:10:46.313 --> 00:11:01.069
A large multicenter trial of three hundred ninety two patients with severe brain injury found no significant improvement in survival or neurologic outcome, and more complications.

77
00:11:01.069 --> 00:11:12.364
If a brain injured patient is cooled, rewarm slowly, because rapid rewarming can bring instability and rebound intracranial hypertension.

78
00:11:12.364 --> 00:11:17.969
The teaching pearl: lowering a number does not mean better recovery.

79
00:11:17.969 --> 00:11:27.450
Hess calls TH likely beneficial in adult brain trauma, but the instructor's deck says not routine, so use the deck.

80
00:11:28.867 --> 00:11:32.354
Temperature management runs in three phases.

81
00:11:32.354 --> 00:11:50.109
Induction: activate the protocol, document the neurologic exam before sedation if possible, start sedation early, begin continuous core temperature and cardiac monitoring, start the cooling method, and treat shivering early.

82
00:11:50.109 --> 00:12:05.644
Maintenance: hold a stable core temperature for at least thirty six hours, with fluctuation no more than about zero point two to zero point five degrees, a suggestion rather than a universal rule.

83
00:12:05.644 --> 00:12:16.344
Monitor electrolytes, glucose, and especially potassium, check the skin under cooling devices, and use EEG when seizures are suspected.

84
00:12:16.344 --> 00:12:27.917
Rewarming: a slow, controlled return toward normal temperature, about zero point two to zero point five degrees per hour, following your protocol.

85
00:12:29.333 --> 00:12:33.277
Rewarming is not just turning the device off.

86
00:12:33.277 --> 00:12:41.164
Vasodilation can cause hypotension and instability, potassium rises, and glucose can fall.

87
00:12:41.164 --> 00:12:47.561
Overshoot is temperature rising above the target, a rebound hyperthermia.

88
00:12:47.561 --> 00:12:54.660
Afterdrop is a further fall in core temperature as cold peripheral blood returns.

89
00:12:54.660 --> 00:13:03.949
Both are why rewarming is gradual, and fever is avoided for seventy two hours after return of circulation.

90
00:13:03.949 --> 00:13:08.506
Shivering occurs in up to forty percent of patients.

91
00:13:08.506 --> 00:13:15.254
It slows cooling and raises oxygen consumption and carbon dioxide production.

92
00:13:15.254 --> 00:13:21.476
Treat it with sedation, anti shivering drugs, and skin counter warming.

93
00:13:21.476 --> 00:13:26.033
Monitored neuromuscular blockade is the last resort.

94
00:13:27.433 --> 00:13:32.080
Cold diuresis is increased urine output during cooling.

95
00:13:32.080 --> 00:13:38.079
The slide gives the cause as increased renal blood flow during cooling.

96
00:13:38.079 --> 00:13:46.527
The notes add the chain: cooling causes peripheral vasoconstriction, which pushes blood to the core.

97
00:13:46.527 --> 00:13:57.341
The heart senses the larger venous return as overload, so atrial natriuretic peptide is released and antidiuretic hormone falls.

98
00:13:57.341 --> 00:14:01.735
The kidneys then make large volumes of dilute urine.

99
00:14:01.735 --> 00:14:12.380
The consequences are loss of fluid, potassium, magnesium, and phosphate, and hypovolemia with less preload and cardiac output.

100
00:14:12.380 --> 00:14:17.533
Monitor volume status and electrolytes, and treat the losses.

101
00:14:18.933 --> 00:14:21.951
The slides contrast the two phases.

102
00:14:21.951 --> 00:14:34.110
During cooling, potassium shifts into the cells and cold diuresis wastes it, so hypokalemia develops, along with low magnesium and phosphate.

103
00:14:34.110 --> 00:14:39.026
Cooling also causes insulin resistance and hyperglycemia.

104
00:14:39.026 --> 00:14:42.130
During rewarming the shifts reverse.

105
00:14:42.130 --> 00:14:54.806
Potassium moves back out of the cells, risking rebound hyperkalemia and dysrhythmias, and insulin sensitivity returns, so hypoglycemia may develop.

106
00:14:54.806 --> 00:14:59.894
That is why potassium is replaced carefully during cooling.

107
00:14:59.894 --> 00:15:13.346
Give potassium chloride by mouth or slowly IV, never undiluted, less than forty milliequivalents in one hour and less than two hundred in twenty four hours.

108
00:15:13.346 --> 00:15:20.072
Replacement stops several hours before rewarming, and potassium is reassessed.

109
00:15:20.072 --> 00:15:25.246
Most protocols keep glucose at eighty to one hundred eighty.

110
00:15:25.246 --> 00:15:33.783
For general potassium normals, use three point eight to five point zero milliequivalents per liter.

111
00:15:35.200 --> 00:15:38.316
Cooling affects nearly every system.

112
00:15:38.316 --> 00:15:56.319
Cardiovascular: bradycardia is expected, a heart rate of forty five to fifty five at thirty three degrees, with higher vascular resistance and possible arrhythmias, especially with low potassium or magnesium.

113
00:15:56.319 --> 00:15:58.915
The ECG can show Osborn waves.

114
00:15:58.915 --> 00:16:15.274
Hess warns that if the temperature drops below the desired range, arrhythmias and coagulopathy follow; serious ventricular arrhythmias go with deeper cooling and low potassium or magnesium.

115
00:16:15.274 --> 00:16:18.563
Hematologic: platelets work less well.

116
00:16:18.563 --> 00:16:29.122
Clotting also slows, but lab clotting tests run at thirty seven degrees and can look near normal while the patient bleeds.

117
00:16:29.122 --> 00:16:32.930
Immune: infection can develop without fever.

118
00:16:32.930 --> 00:16:39.941
Gut and liver: slow emptying and slower drug clearance, so sedatives last longer.

119
00:16:39.941 --> 00:16:49.375
Respiratory: less carbon dioxide production and weaker cough and secretion clearance, raising pneumonia risk.

120
00:16:50.800 --> 00:16:59.401
The Glasgow Coma Scale adds three parts: eye opening up to four, verbal up to five, and motor up to six.

121
00:16:59.401 --> 00:17:05.273
The maximum is fifteen and the minimum is three, and lower means worse.

122
00:17:05.273 --> 00:17:11.642
The instructor stresses that motor response best predicts neurologic outcome.

123
00:17:11.642 --> 00:17:18.341
Use mild thirteen to fifteen, moderate nine to twelve, and severe three to eight.

124
00:17:18.341 --> 00:17:28.513
A score of eight or less prompts you to consider intubation for airway protection, a guideline rather than an absolute law.

125
00:17:28.513 --> 00:17:34.964
In intubated patients verbal cannot be tested, so the range is two T to ten T.

126
00:17:34.964 --> 00:17:41.250
Heavy sedation or paralysis can lower the score without severe brain injury.

127
00:17:42.667 --> 00:17:51.415
The blood gas analyzer always measures at thirty seven degrees, whatever the patient's temperature.

128
00:17:51.415 --> 00:17:59.632
Gas solubility is inversely related to temperature, so colder blood holds more dissolved gas.

129
00:17:59.632 --> 00:18:12.533
In a cooled patient, the real PaO2 and PaCO2 are lower and the pH is higher than the thirty seven degree printout, so the patient looks alkalotic.

130
00:18:12.533 --> 00:18:15.361
Warm patients show the opposite.

131
00:18:15.361 --> 00:18:22.076
The slide's advice is to understand the direction, not memorize the numbers.

132
00:18:22.076 --> 00:18:28.968
Oxygen content does not change, so a low corrected PaO2 is not true hypoxemia.

133
00:18:28.968 --> 00:18:33.917
Cooling also shifts the oxyhemoglobin curve to the left.

134
00:18:35.333 --> 00:18:47.610
For every degree below thirty seven, the TH slides give pH plus zero point zero one five, PaCO2 minus two, and PaO2 minus five.

135
00:18:47.610 --> 00:18:59.113
Written as formulas: corrected PaO2 equals measured PaO2 minus five times thirty seven minus the patient's temperature.

136
00:18:59.113 --> 00:19:08.683
Corrected PaCO2 uses two, and corrected pH adds zero point zero one five times the same difference.

137
00:19:08.683 --> 00:19:20.187
Hess's example: a patient at thirty three degrees with a printout of pH seven point four, PaCO2 forty, and PaO2 ninety.

138
00:19:20.187 --> 00:19:22.410
The difference is four.

139
00:19:22.410 --> 00:19:33.333
PaCO2 becomes thirty two, pH becomes seven point four six, and PaO2 becomes seventy, close to Hess's sixty eight.

140
00:19:34.733 --> 00:19:39.573
The sources disagree on whether to temperature correct.

141
00:19:39.573 --> 00:19:48.461
The TH slides say correction is not automatically required: follow the prescribed blood gas strategy.

142
00:19:48.461 --> 00:19:57.789
Alpha stat uses the uncorrected thirty seven degree values, and pH stat uses temperature corrected values.

143
00:19:57.789 --> 00:20:09.845
The RCP 105 Monitoring deck says correct to the core temperature, and RCP 203 says always document the patient's temperature at sampling.

144
00:20:09.845 --> 00:20:21.549
Hess says correction is unnecessary and that any corrected values must be labeled, with the thirty seven degree values also reported.

145
00:20:21.549 --> 00:20:32.108
For the instructor's exam, give the slide's answer, follow the prescribed strategy, and know the per degree corrections.

146
00:20:33.533 --> 00:20:46.151
The deck's chain is: lower temperature, lower metabolism, lower carbon dioxide production, changed ABG interpretation, and ventilator changes.

147
00:20:46.151 --> 00:20:57.435
If minute ventilation stays the same while the patient cools, PaCO2 falls, and cerebral vasoconstriction cuts brain blood flow.

148
00:20:57.435 --> 00:21:01.789
Adjust often using ABGs and end tidal CO2 trends.

149
00:21:01.789 --> 00:21:10.764
During rewarming carbon dioxide production rises again, so minute ventilation may need to go back up.

150
00:21:10.764 --> 00:21:25.158
The slide targets are PaCO2 thirty five to forty five, PaO2 sixty to one hundred five after return of circulation, and mean arterial pressure at least sixty five.

151
00:21:25.158 --> 00:21:31.733
The older notes list thirty five to forty and eighty to one hundred fifty.

152
00:21:33.133 --> 00:21:39.781
A higher PaCO2 dilates cerebral vessels and raises intracranial pressure.

153
00:21:39.781 --> 00:21:46.064
A lower PaCO2 constricts them, lowering pressure but also blood flow.

154
00:21:46.064 --> 00:21:50.162
Keep PaCO2 normal, thirty five to forty five.

155
00:21:50.162 --> 00:22:00.178
Routine hyperventilation is no longer recommended because too much vasoconstriction can worsen brain ischemia.

156
00:22:00.178 --> 00:22:12.654
Temporary hyperventilation to about thirty to thirty five is only for an emergency rise in intracranial pressure or impending herniation.

157
00:22:12.654 --> 00:22:23.308
The RCP 105 slide allows PaCO2 as low as twenty six in the first twenty four hours, with no added benefit below that.

158
00:22:23.308 --> 00:22:34.508
For the brain injured patient, also sedate to prevent pressure spikes, keep PaO2 above sixty, and suction only when needed.

159
00:22:35.933 --> 00:22:41.271
Several equipment and monitoring cautions come with a cooled patient.

160
00:22:41.271 --> 00:22:50.321
Do not use a heat and moisture exchanger in a hypothermic patient, because it reduces the exhaled humidity available.

161
00:22:50.321 --> 00:23:07.803
Hess says to set the ventilator humidifier to the patient's target core temperature, while the TH slide notes say humidified oxygen, not heated circuits; the deck does not reconcile these, so match the wording of the question.

162
00:23:07.803 --> 00:23:17.162
Cooling and sedation blunt cough and secretion clearance, so plan airway clearance and watch for pneumonia without fever.

163
00:23:17.162 --> 00:23:23.041
Handle the airway gently, since suctioning and arterial punctures can bleed.

164
00:23:23.041 --> 00:23:28.533
Transcutaneous oxygen accuracy is affected, and weaning may be delayed.

165
00:23:29.933 --> 00:23:38.963
A patient cooled to thirty two degrees has an ABG run at thirty seven that shows a PaCO2 of forty five.

166
00:23:38.963 --> 00:23:48.343
In which direction are the patient's real PaCO2 and pH compared with the printout, and roughly by how much?

167
00:23:48.343 --> 00:23:49.833
Pause and answer.

168
00:23:50.733 --> 00:23:56.733
[6-second practice pause]

169
00:23:57.233 --> 00:24:07.993
Colder blood holds more dissolved gas, so the patient's real PaCO2 is lower than the printout and the real pH is higher.

170
00:24:07.993 --> 00:24:12.387
The difference from thirty seven is five degrees.

171
00:24:12.387 --> 00:24:19.470
PaCO2 falls by two per degree, so forty five minus ten gives about thirty five.

172
00:24:19.470 --> 00:24:28.078
The pH rises by zero point zero one five per degree, so about zero point zero seven five higher.

173
00:24:28.078 --> 00:24:33.458
The patient looks more alkalotic than the printout suggests.

174
00:24:34.867 --> 00:24:43.847
Your patient has been cooled for thirty six hours and was given potassium for hypokalemia during cooling.

175
00:24:43.847 --> 00:24:46.242
Rewarming is about to start.

176
00:24:46.242 --> 00:24:56.163
Which way do potassium and glucose tend to move during rewarming, and what does that mean for potassium replacement?

177
00:24:56.163 --> 00:24:57.617
Pause and answer.

178
00:24:58.533 --> 00:25:04.533
[6-second practice pause]

179
00:25:05.033 --> 00:25:18.749
During rewarming, potassium shifts back out of the cells, so it can rise into rebound hyperkalemia with dysrhythmias, which is why replacement during cooling is done carefully.

180
00:25:18.749 --> 00:25:23.971
Insulin sensitivity returns, so glucose can fall into hypoglycemia.

181
00:25:23.971 --> 00:25:38.933
That is why potassium replacement is stopped several hours before rewarming, potassium is reassessed before and during rewarming, and glucose is checked every one to two hours while rewarming.

182
00:25:40.333 --> 00:25:41.121
To recap.

183
00:25:41.121 --> 00:26:01.504
Current practice is a constant thirty two to thirty seven point five degrees for at least thirty six hours in adults unresponsive after return of circulation, with a mean arterial pressure of at least sixty five and fever prevention.

184
00:26:01.504 --> 00:26:07.015
The trial used thirty two to thirty four for twenty four hours.

185
00:26:07.015 --> 00:26:16.114
Cooling lowers brain metabolism and may blunt the injury cascade, but it is not routine in brain trauma.

186
00:26:16.114 --> 00:26:28.099
Cooling brings cold diuresis, low potassium, and high glucose; rewarming brings rebound high potassium and low glucose, so rewarm slowly.

187
00:26:28.099 --> 00:26:35.447
The analyzer reads at thirty seven, so interpret the gases with temperature in mind.

188
00:26:35.447 --> 00:26:42.533
Keep PaCO2 thirty five to forty five and adjust ventilation as the patient cools.
